{"chunk_id": "edbc2aa0c914", "text": "Krishna explains the nature of divine reality and devotion. He describes two aspects of his nature: a lower material nature composed of elements such as earth, water, fire, mind, and intellect, and a higher living principle that sustains the universe. Everything arises from him and is held together like pearls on a string. Though he pervades all qualities of nature\u2014goodness, passion, and darkness\u2014he transcends them, and the world, deluded by these qualities, fails to recognize him. Different kinds of people worship him, but the wise person who knows that \"Vasudeva is all\" is exceedingly rare. The passage then moves to Arjuna\u2019s questions about Brahman and related concepts. Krishna teaches that one\u2019s final thought at death shapes one\u2019s destination, and that steady meditation on the divine leads to union with the Supreme.\nKrishna explains his cosmic nature, the illusion of the three qualities, forms of devotion, and how remembrance of him at death leads to union.\nThemes: divine immanence, maya and three gunas, forms of devotion, remembrance at death, cosmic source of universe", "vector": [0.015301265753805637, 0.01261215005069971, -0.001563496422022581, -0.00022646687284577638, -0.018296407535672188, 0.013816718012094498, -0.03239961713552475, -0.018296407535672188, -0.03661885857582092, -0.008067348040640354, -0.0004887452232651412, 0.015457534231245518, -0.013465113937854767, 0.007429252844303846, -0.01856987737119198, 0.005342420656234026, 0.0484171137213707, -0.010144413448870182, 0.022906320169568062, -0.023270945996046066, 0.00046066578943282366, 0.0057982029393315315, -0.0006621053325943649, 0.005433577112853527, -0.010522061958909035, 0.013282801024615765, 0.0017629011999815702, -0.027503211051225662, 0.0161867868155241, -0.017853647470474243, 3.0241364584071562e-05, 0.006039116531610489, -0.010723908431828022, -0.0023342568892985582, -0.017020216211676598, 0.02015860378742218, -0.00426482083275